<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Donar, Thunor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thor was the Germanic god of thunder, known to the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> as Thunaer or Donar and to the English as Thunor. Armed with his hammer Mjolnir, he was the protector of gods and men against giants and the most widely worshipped god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. The Old Saxon baptismal vow required converts to renounce him, and his name survives in Thursday.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thor.jpg" alt="Thor" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Max Koch · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_painting_by_Max_Friedrich_Koch_-_Donar-Thor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Thor's name descends from the Proto-Germanic *Þun(a)raz, meaning thunder, and its forms include Old English Thunor, Old Saxon Thunar and Old High German Donar. He closely resembles other Indo-European thunder gods, among them the Celtic Taranis, the Baltic Perkūnas, the Slavic Perun and the Hindu Indra, and scholars compare Indra's slaying of Vritra with Thor's battle with the serpent Jörmungandr. For this reason he is generally accepted as ultimately derived from a Proto-Indo-European deity. Roman writers identified him with Jupiter or Hercules, and the weekday name Thursday is a calque of the Latin Iovis dies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In Norse mythology Thor is a god associated with thunder, storms, strength, protection, fertility and farmers. He is the son of Odin and Jörð and the husband of the golden-haired Sif. He rides in a cart pulled by two goats, Tanngrisnir and Tanngnjóstr, which he eats and brings back to life, and he wields the hammer Mjölnir, wears the belt Megingjörð and the iron gloves Járngreipr. He is foretold to kill Jörmungandr at Ragnarök and then to take only nine steps before dying of its venom.</p><p>The earliest record of the name is on the Nordendorf fibulae from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, which bear the name Þonar in Elder Futhark. A 9th-century codex from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, the Old Saxon Baptismal Vow, lists Thunaer among three gods to be renounced. According to a near-contemporary account, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> felled an oak dedicated to Jove, known as Donar's Oak, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> in the 8th century.</p><p>In the 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> wrote that a statue of Thor stood at the centre of a triple throne in the temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>, and that sacrifices were offered to him when plague or famine threatened. Around 1000 hammer pendants have been found in the Nordic countries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Baltic countries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and Personal names containing his name are recorded with great frequency only from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Thor was still invoked in the 12th century, a century after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was officially Christianized, as a runic stick from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> shows. His name survives in place names such as Þórslundr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and in Thundersley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Scandinavian folklore kept words such as tordön for thunder, and a belief that lightning frightens away trolls. In the modern period he has been the subject of poems by writers including Adam Gottlob Oehlenschläger and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</a>, and veneration of him has been revived in Heathenry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion was pagan: a Saxon baptismal vow of the late 8th or 9th century makes converts renounce <b>Thunaer</b>, Woden and Saxnot, and sacred sites included springs, groves and the Irminsul, a great pillar or tree near the Eresburg, destroyed by Charlemagne in 772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The storm god Tarhunt fought the serpent Illuyanka, a conflict that resembles that of Indra and Vritra in Vedic mythology and of <b>Thor</b> and Jörmungandr in Norse mythology.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hittite-religion">Hittite religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>A soapstone mould found in Denmark, from the 10th century, was made to cast both Christian crosses and the hammers of <b>Thor</b>, so that a smith could sell to customers of either faith.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Sámi religion shares some elements with Norse mythology, possibly from early contact with Viking traders, and contemporary ethnographers counted Sámi among the last worshippers of <b>Thor</b> in the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>…and kings, who held sacrificial feasts, blót, at their halls and at sacred places, and the gods were many, among them Odin, god of wisdom, poetry and the dead in battle, <b>Thor</b>, the thunderer who guarded gods and people, and Freyr and Freyja, who gave fertility and plenty.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>After 101 days and some 7,000 km from Peru, <b>Thor</b> Heyerdahl's balsa raft ran onto the reef at Raroia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
